1
00:00:01,075 --> 00:00:01,758
On day 61,

2
00:00:01,758 --> 00:00:04,117
the truck is still outside your gate.

3
00:00:04,117 --> 00:00:05,250
Yesterday,

4
00:00:05,250 --> 00:00:07,121
you asked for one night to decide.

5
00:00:07,121 --> 00:00:08,943
The woman on the radio said one

6
00:00:08,943 --> 00:00:10,896
night was all the crowd would give you.

7
00:00:10,896 --> 00:00:12,166
Then she switched off her

8
00:00:12,166 --> 00:00:14,136
engine and slept in her cab.

9
00:00:14,136 --> 00:00:16,909
That is either trust or a very good bluff.

10
00:00:16,909 --> 00:00:18,443
Your name is Patch.

11
00:00:18,834 --> 00:00:19,728
Sixty days ago,

12
00:00:19,728 --> 00:00:21,654
you were waiting for a bus.

13
00:00:21,654 --> 00:00:24,405
Since then, you have lived under a bus

14
00:00:24,405 --> 00:00:26,538
depot with Nora, who used to clean it.

15
00:00:26,538 --> 00:00:28,956
You emptied the yard with your trash.

16
00:00:28,956 --> 00:00:31,416
You locked eleven walkers in a coach.

17
00:00:31,416 --> 00:00:32,786
You hung a sign over the

18
00:00:32,786 --> 00:00:34,869
street that says TWO ALIVE.

19
00:00:34,869 --> 00:00:36,334
Somebody read it.

20
00:00:36,334 --> 00:00:38,102
At first light,

21
00:00:38,102 --> 00:00:41,779
Nora climbs to the gantry and comes back down fast.

22
00:00:41,779 --> 00:00:44,495
Figures are walking back up the street.

23
00:00:44,495 --> 00:00:45,338
Slowly.

24
00:00:45,697 --> 00:00:48,355
The way the dead do everything.

25
00:00:48,355 --> 00:00:50,975
"Now or never," she whispers.

26
00:00:50,975 --> 00:00:52,801
You both know this gate.

27
00:00:52,801 --> 00:00:54,569
It does not open.

28
00:00:54,569 --> 00:00:55,856
It screams.

29
00:00:55,856 --> 00:00:59,608
So you agree on a gap exactly one person wide,

30
00:00:59,608 --> 00:01:02,008
and not a centimeter more.

31
00:01:02,008 --> 00:01:03,917
You lift the bolt and pull.

32
00:01:03,917 --> 00:01:06,220
The gate shrieks along its rail,

33
00:01:06,220 --> 00:01:09,454
and down the street, every head turns.

34
00:01:09,454 --> 00:01:11,822
A man squeezes through sideways,

35
00:01:11,822 --> 00:01:15,039
hugging a cardboard box to his chest.

36
00:01:15,039 --> 00:01:16,815
He wears a red knit hat,

37
00:01:16,815 --> 00:01:20,937
and a small round mirror hangs from a cord around his neck.

38
00:01:21,472 --> 00:01:23,224
You shove the gate back.

39
00:01:23,224 --> 00:01:24,486
It screams again.

40
00:01:24,486 --> 00:01:25,858
The bolt drops.

41
00:01:25,858 --> 00:01:28,435
Outside, an engine starts.

42
00:01:28,874 --> 00:01:30,780
One short blast of the horn.

43
00:01:30,780 --> 00:01:33,873
Then the woman's voice, on the radio.

44
00:01:33,873 --> 00:01:37,051
"I don't go into places with one way out.

45
00:01:37,051 --> 00:01:38,023
Wes does.

46
00:01:38,023 --> 00:01:39,480
He'll explain.

47
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:41,970
He's had a week of practice."

48
00:01:41,970 --> 00:01:44,444
The engine rolls away down the street,

49
00:01:44,755 --> 00:01:46,979
and the footsteps turn to follow it.

50
00:01:46,979 --> 00:01:49,657
The man in the red hat turns to you.

51
00:01:49,657 --> 00:01:51,834
"Hello," he whispers.

52
00:01:51,834 --> 00:01:54,121
"I'm the explanation."

53
00:01:54,121 --> 00:01:56,165
In the box, there is a can of

54
00:01:56,165 --> 00:01:58,422
oil and two packs of batteries.

55
00:01:58,422 --> 00:02:00,815
Before anyone can stop him,

56
00:02:00,815 --> 00:02:02,433
Wes kneels at the gate wheel

57
00:02:02,433 --> 00:02:04,405
and pours half the can onto it.

58
00:02:04,405 --> 00:02:07,090
"Housewarming present," he says.

59
00:02:07,090 --> 00:02:10,535
"I've heard about your gate."

60
00:02:10,535 --> 00:02:13,747
Days 62 and 63 teach you that a

61
00:02:13,747 --> 00:02:16,119
bunker for two is a bunker for two.

62
00:02:16,701 --> 00:02:19,275
Wes sleeps on the stairs below the steel door,

63
00:02:19,275 --> 00:02:21,029
with his hat over his eyes.

64
00:02:21,029 --> 00:02:22,632
He snores so softly that

65
00:02:22,632 --> 00:02:24,295
Nora decides to allow it.

66
00:02:24,295 --> 00:02:25,724
He studies your chart.

67
00:02:25,724 --> 00:02:26,842
Days on the left,

68
00:02:26,842 --> 00:02:28,660
walkers gone on the right.

69
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:31,012
"You need a third column," he says.

70
00:02:31,385 --> 00:02:32,342
"People met."

71
00:02:32,342 --> 00:02:33,299
You add it.

72
00:02:33,299 --> 00:02:35,153
It has one mark.

73
00:02:35,153 --> 00:02:37,102
Then he eats a sugared almond,

74
00:02:37,102 --> 00:02:39,594
closes his eyes, and says it is the

75
00:02:39,594 --> 00:02:42,056
nicest wedding he has ever been to.

76
00:02:42,447 --> 00:02:45,259
The truck took him off his roof on day 52.

77
00:02:45,259 --> 00:02:47,635
It belongs to about a dozen people

78
00:02:47,635 --> 00:02:50,095
living at the old waterworks on the hilltop,

79
00:02:50,095 --> 00:02:53,094
behind a fence, beside tanks of clean water.

80
00:02:53,693 --> 00:02:55,284
Every morning, they drive out and

81
00:02:55,284 --> 00:02:57,175
herd the dead away from the hill.

82
00:02:57,871 --> 00:02:59,021
"Like you," Wes says.

83
00:02:59,021 --> 00:03:01,448
"Only with a horn instead of rubbish."

84
00:03:02,092 --> 00:03:04,371
On day 63, he tells you the rest.

85
00:03:04,371 --> 00:03:05,983
From the hilltop,

86
00:03:05,983 --> 00:03:08,059
you can see the motorway to the city.

87
00:03:08,059 --> 00:03:08,715
For two weeks,

88
00:03:08,715 --> 00:03:10,667
something has been moving along it.

89
00:03:10,667 --> 00:03:13,169
The people on the hill call it the river.

90
00:03:13,531 --> 00:03:14,758
Thousands of the dead,

91
00:03:14,758 --> 00:03:18,748
all walking the same way, at the speed of a very slow queue.

92
00:03:18,748 --> 00:03:21,310
"How long before it gets here?"

93
00:03:21,310 --> 00:03:22,204
Nora asks.

94
00:03:22,204 --> 00:03:24,049
"Four weeks," says Wes.

95
00:03:24,409 --> 00:03:26,129
"Maybe less."

96
00:03:26,129 --> 00:03:27,840
On day 64,

97
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:30,259
with fresh batteries, the radio can finally run

98
00:03:30,259 --> 00:03:32,133
for longer than five minutes.

99
00:03:32,133 --> 00:03:33,654
The woman uses every second.

100
00:03:33,654 --> 00:03:35,580
She still won't give her name.

101
00:03:36,131 --> 00:03:37,218
Wes says she thinks

102
00:03:37,218 --> 00:03:38,902
names are for people who stay.

103
00:03:38,902 --> 00:03:40,104
Her plan is simple.

104
00:03:40,104 --> 00:03:42,050
Where the motorway reaches town,

105
00:03:42,050 --> 00:03:43,599
the road splits.

106
00:03:43,599 --> 00:03:44,812
Uphill to her people,

107
00:03:44,812 --> 00:03:46,356
or downhill past your gate.

108
00:03:46,356 --> 00:03:48,957
Her truck can lead the river to the split.

109
00:03:48,957 --> 00:03:51,243
Something else has to call it down.

110
00:03:51,243 --> 00:03:52,236
Something loud that

111
00:03:52,236 --> 00:03:53,727
never runs out of fuel.

112
00:03:54,454 --> 00:03:56,615
You and Nora look up at the same moment.

113
00:03:56,615 --> 00:03:57,906
Above the ceiling,

114
00:03:57,906 --> 00:04:00,526
under the canopy, sits the loudspeaker you

115
00:04:00,526 --> 00:04:02,727
spent two weeks trying to shut up.

116
00:04:02,727 --> 00:04:05,995
"Your yard has walls," the woman says.

117
00:04:05,995 --> 00:04:07,064
"Your gate locks.

118
00:04:07,064 --> 00:04:09,313
Your speaker runs on sunlight.

119
00:04:09,623 --> 00:04:10,593
Call them in.

120
00:04:10,593 --> 00:04:11,703
Close the gate.

121
00:04:11,703 --> 00:04:13,657
Then come up the hill.

122
00:04:13,657 --> 00:04:14,402
Food.

123
00:04:14,402 --> 00:04:16,014
Clean water.

124
00:04:16,014 --> 00:04:17,255
Daylight."

125
00:04:17,255 --> 00:04:19,617
"A bunker for a view," says Wes,

126
00:04:19,617 --> 00:04:21,129
who is trying to help.

127
00:04:21,129 --> 00:04:23,556
That night, you argue in whispers.

128
00:04:23,556 --> 00:04:24,958
You want the hill.

129
00:04:25,444 --> 00:04:26,161
Sunlight.

130
00:04:26,161 --> 00:04:27,165
Other voices.

131
00:04:27,165 --> 00:04:28,455
A door that shuts

132
00:04:28,455 --> 00:04:30,373
without a desk behind it.

133
00:04:30,373 --> 00:04:33,186
Nora is quiet for a long time.

134
00:04:33,186 --> 00:04:35,142
Then: "She wants to put the

135
00:04:35,142 --> 00:04:37,397
whole city on our ceiling."

136
00:04:38,090 --> 00:04:40,923
On day 65, the woman sets a deadline.

137
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:42,152
"Day 70.

138
00:04:42,152 --> 00:04:43,896
Have an answer."

139
00:04:43,896 --> 00:04:46,893
On day 66, Nora walks out to the coach.

140
00:04:46,893 --> 00:04:48,194
She sits on the concrete

141
00:04:48,194 --> 00:04:49,545
beside the folding door.

142
00:04:49,545 --> 00:04:50,488
Behind the glass,

143
00:04:50,488 --> 00:04:52,635
the man in the blue depot vest stands at

144
00:04:52,635 --> 00:04:54,436
the top of the aisle, facing her.

145
00:04:54,436 --> 00:04:56,581
He turns whenever she comes close.

146
00:04:56,924 --> 00:04:58,726
You sit down next to her.

147
00:04:58,726 --> 00:04:59,419
"Up there,

148
00:04:59,419 --> 00:05:02,692
I'm a stranger with a teal bag," she says.

149
00:05:02,692 --> 00:05:05,212
"Down here, I know where every key is.

150
00:05:05,212 --> 00:05:06,617
Which stair creaks.

151
00:05:06,617 --> 00:05:08,532
Which locker was mine.

152
00:05:08,987 --> 00:05:10,314
I've kept this building

153
00:05:10,314 --> 00:05:12,083
going longer than it's kept me."

154
00:05:12,083 --> 00:05:13,904
She nods at the coach.

155
00:05:13,904 --> 00:05:15,401
"And if we fill the yard,

156
00:05:15,401 --> 00:05:18,418
he gets the whole city for passengers.

157
00:05:18,418 --> 00:05:20,070
He'd have hated that."

158
00:05:20,070 --> 00:05:21,348
You don't argue.

159
00:05:21,348 --> 00:05:24,387
Some arguments are lost before they start.

160
00:05:24,387 --> 00:05:27,138
On day 67, the pounding comes back.

161
00:05:27,138 --> 00:05:29,514
The crowd the truck keeps leading

162
00:05:29,514 --> 00:05:31,663
away has drifted home to your gate,

163
00:05:31,663 --> 00:05:34,428
the way rainwater finds the lowest street.

164
00:05:34,428 --> 00:05:35,942
From the gantry,

165
00:05:35,942 --> 00:05:39,883
you watch the first one arrive: a walker in a ragged olive vest,

166
00:05:39,883 --> 00:05:41,189
who walks straight up to

167
00:05:41,189 --> 00:05:43,502
the gate and puts both hands on it,

168
00:05:43,502 --> 00:05:45,945
like a man waiting to be buzzed in.

169
00:05:45,945 --> 00:05:49,384
By evening, forty more stand behind him.

170
00:05:49,384 --> 00:05:51,929
Wes names him the Doorman.

171
00:05:51,929 --> 00:05:53,564
You wish he hadn't.

172
00:05:53,564 --> 00:05:55,261
Now he's somebody.

173
00:05:55,261 --> 00:05:58,828
By day 68, Wes has inspected everything

174
00:05:58,828 --> 00:06:01,475
you own and found most of it disappointing.

175
00:06:01,475 --> 00:06:03,548
He stops at the bay door.

176
00:06:03,548 --> 00:06:04,700
The snapped latch.

177
00:06:04,700 --> 00:06:05,883
The desk.

178
00:06:05,883 --> 00:06:07,926
The trolley wedged behind it.

179
00:06:07,926 --> 00:06:08,880
"Who did this?"

180
00:06:08,880 --> 00:06:09,793
he asks.

181
00:06:10,151 --> 00:06:11,908
"Management," says Nora.

182
00:06:11,908 --> 00:06:14,960
So Wes takes the latch off the office door,

183
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:16,393
which has had nothing to

184
00:06:16,393 --> 00:06:17,997
protect for seven weeks,

185
00:06:17,997 --> 00:06:20,575
and spends two days fitting it to the bay door.

186
00:06:21,109 --> 00:06:22,282
One screw at a time,

187
00:06:22,282 --> 00:06:24,313
each with its own tiny squeak.

188
00:06:24,912 --> 00:06:25,958
Nora holds the

189
00:06:25,958 --> 00:06:28,188
flashlight and barely breathes.

190
00:06:28,188 --> 00:06:30,504
On day 69, the bay door clicks shut on

191
00:06:30,504 --> 00:06:33,025
its own for the first time since day 39.

192
00:06:33,543 --> 00:06:34,733
Nora pushes it.

193
00:06:34,733 --> 00:06:35,477
It holds.

194
00:06:35,477 --> 00:06:37,313
She pushes again,

195
00:06:37,313 --> 00:06:39,663
harder, the way you test good news.

196
00:06:39,663 --> 00:06:41,664
"Management said they'd

197
00:06:41,664 --> 00:06:43,699
replace this," she says.

198
00:06:43,699 --> 00:06:45,186
Sixty-two days late.

199
00:06:45,575 --> 00:06:46,694
But management came.

200
00:06:46,694 --> 00:06:48,134
You leave the desk and the

201
00:06:48,134 --> 00:06:50,316
trolley where they are anyway.

202
00:06:50,316 --> 00:06:52,598
Some trust takes longer to install.

203
00:06:53,036 --> 00:06:54,511
Day 70 is the deadline.

204
00:06:54,511 --> 00:06:57,811
You and Nora make the decision the night before,

205
00:06:57,811 --> 00:06:59,017
on the bench in the dark.

206
00:06:59,017 --> 00:07:00,752
You won't go up the hill.

207
00:07:00,752 --> 00:07:02,065
She won't keep the yard.

208
00:07:02,065 --> 00:07:03,588
It is the first argument you

209
00:07:03,588 --> 00:07:04,529
have both lost,

210
00:07:04,529 --> 00:07:07,154
and somehow it feels like the right result.

211
00:07:07,641 --> 00:07:09,652
At mid-morning, the truck comes back,

212
00:07:09,652 --> 00:07:12,487
leads the Doorman's crowd around the block,

213
00:07:12,487 --> 00:07:13,914
then returns alone and parks

214
00:07:13,914 --> 00:07:16,825
against the depot wall, right under your sign.

215
00:07:16,825 --> 00:07:17,814
"You can have the

216
00:07:17,814 --> 00:07:19,326
yard," you tell the radio.

217
00:07:19,326 --> 00:07:21,259
"You can't have the depot.

218
00:07:21,259 --> 00:07:22,009
We stay."

219
00:07:22,009 --> 00:07:24,199
There is a long pause.

220
00:07:24,199 --> 00:07:25,857
"Fair," she says.

221
00:07:25,857 --> 00:07:28,065
Two silhouettes climb

222
00:07:28,065 --> 00:07:29,263
onto the roof of the truck.

223
00:07:29,263 --> 00:07:31,522
You lower a rope from the end of the gantry,

224
00:07:31,522 --> 00:07:34,014
they tie sacks to it, and you haul.

225
00:07:34,014 --> 00:07:34,862
Tins.

226
00:07:34,862 --> 00:07:35,773
Crackers.

227
00:07:35,773 --> 00:07:37,049
Ration packs.

228
00:07:37,049 --> 00:07:39,702
Four big jugs of water from the hill.

229
00:07:39,702 --> 00:07:40,701
More batteries.

230
00:07:40,701 --> 00:07:42,250
Another can of oil.

231
00:07:42,559 --> 00:07:44,561
A first-aid kit full of bandages.

232
00:07:44,561 --> 00:07:46,712
Every one of them is blue.

233
00:07:46,712 --> 00:07:48,190
You keep the orange one on.

234
00:07:48,190 --> 00:07:50,653
You have been through too much together.

235
00:07:51,058 --> 00:07:52,820
Wes could leave with the truck.

236
00:07:52,820 --> 00:07:55,047
He looks at the cab, then at Nora.

237
00:07:55,533 --> 00:07:56,945
"I spent two months as a

238
00:07:56,945 --> 00:07:59,106
very obvious sheep," he says.

239
00:07:59,624 --> 00:08:02,187
"I'd like to try being the dog."

240
00:08:02,187 --> 00:08:04,547
"Day 90," says the woman.

241
00:08:04,547 --> 00:08:05,612
"Give or take."

242
00:08:05,612 --> 00:08:07,364
The engine pulls away.

243
00:08:07,364 --> 00:08:09,057
A hand waves once from

244
00:08:09,057 --> 00:08:11,481
the cab window, and is gone.

245
00:08:11,481 --> 00:08:14,879
From day 71 to day 75,

246
00:08:14,879 --> 00:08:17,553
you build a trap with yourselves inside it.

247
00:08:17,553 --> 00:08:19,295
All the food and water

248
00:08:19,295 --> 00:08:20,335
go down to the bunker.

249
00:08:20,335 --> 00:08:21,737
The suitcases from the coach's

250
00:08:21,737 --> 00:08:24,059
luggage hold go up against the bay door,

251
00:08:24,059 --> 00:08:25,819
behind the desk and the trolley.

252
00:08:26,274 --> 00:08:28,403
A wall of other people's holidays.

253
00:08:28,403 --> 00:08:30,788
Nora fetches the canopy ladder

254
00:08:30,788 --> 00:08:32,041
from the maintenance cupboard.

255
00:08:32,041 --> 00:08:33,497
She used to climb it once a year to

256
00:08:33,497 --> 00:08:36,494
clean the gutters, and she hated every rung.

257
00:08:36,494 --> 00:08:39,011
You lash it to the far end of the gantry,

258
00:08:39,011 --> 00:08:39,906
where it reaches

259
00:08:39,906 --> 00:08:42,214
down into the yard beside the gate.

260
00:08:42,214 --> 00:08:43,624
Whoever closes the gate will

261
00:08:43,624 --> 00:08:45,787
need a way out above the crowd.

262
00:08:45,787 --> 00:08:46,776
That will be you.

263
00:08:46,776 --> 00:08:48,749
When the gate slides open,

264
00:08:48,749 --> 00:08:51,845
its heavy panel lies flat against the inside of the wall,

265
00:08:51,845 --> 00:08:54,195
with a narrow slot between them.

266
00:08:54,195 --> 00:08:55,879
You practice standing in it,

267
00:08:55,879 --> 00:08:58,564
the orange bag held against your chest.

268
00:08:58,564 --> 00:09:00,056
It is the smallest room you

269
00:09:00,056 --> 00:09:01,243
have ever lived in,

270
00:09:01,243 --> 00:09:03,837
and you have lived in some small rooms.

271
00:09:04,354 --> 00:09:06,319
Wes will stay at the control box.

272
00:09:06,319 --> 00:09:08,524
Nora will stand at the top of

273
00:09:08,524 --> 00:09:10,061
the ladder with the flashlight.

274
00:09:10,061 --> 00:09:11,609
One flash means close it.

275
00:09:12,127 --> 00:09:13,886
On day 74, Nora climbs out to the

276
00:09:13,886 --> 00:09:16,566
sign with the last of the yellow paint.

277
00:09:16,566 --> 00:09:19,008
She crosses out TWO and writes THREE.

278
00:09:19,527 --> 00:09:20,694
Wes pretends not to

279
00:09:20,694 --> 00:09:22,328
notice that she is pleased.

280
00:09:22,328 --> 00:09:23,872
On the bunker wall,

281
00:09:23,872 --> 00:09:25,338
the chart grows a fourth column.

282
00:09:25,338 --> 00:09:26,713
Days until the river.

283
00:09:27,135 --> 00:09:28,247
It is the first column you

284
00:09:28,247 --> 00:09:29,907
have ever kept that counts down.

285
00:09:30,442 --> 00:09:32,351
On the night of day 76,

286
00:09:32,351 --> 00:09:35,522
you wake up, and Wes is not on the stairs.

287
00:09:35,522 --> 00:09:37,069
He is at the top of them,

288
00:09:37,069 --> 00:09:39,867
by the steel door, whispering into the radio.

289
00:09:39,867 --> 00:09:41,593
You only catch the end.

290
00:09:41,919 --> 00:09:42,293
"No.

291
00:09:42,293 --> 00:09:44,254
I haven't told them."

292
00:09:44,254 --> 00:09:46,413
You lie very still.

293
00:09:46,413 --> 00:09:48,637
You have been betrayed by a latch,

294
00:09:48,637 --> 00:09:50,589
the rain and a trolley.

295
00:09:50,589 --> 00:09:52,645
It never occurred to you that a

296
00:09:52,645 --> 00:09:55,572
man in a red hat could join the list.

297
00:09:55,572 --> 00:09:57,420
In the morning, you ask him.

298
00:09:57,420 --> 00:09:58,682
Not loudly.

299
00:09:58,682 --> 00:10:00,276
Nothing down here is loud.

300
00:10:00,276 --> 00:10:01,634
Wes takes off his hat,

301
00:10:01,634 --> 00:10:04,115
which you have never seen him do.

302
00:10:04,115 --> 00:10:05,103
Underneath,

303
00:10:05,103 --> 00:10:08,266
his head is exactly as round as yours.

304
00:10:08,266 --> 00:10:09,483
Then he tells you.

305
00:10:09,483 --> 00:10:11,681
The hill counted the river again.

306
00:10:11,681 --> 00:10:13,247
It is not thousands.

307
00:10:13,620 --> 00:10:14,991
It is many thousands.

308
00:10:14,991 --> 00:10:17,160
More than your yard can hold.

309
00:10:17,549 --> 00:10:19,342
The speaker can take the front of it.

310
00:10:19,342 --> 00:10:21,444
The truck will try to lead the rest away.

311
00:10:21,444 --> 00:10:22,861
But if the tail is still

312
00:10:22,861 --> 00:10:25,135
coming when your yard is full,

313
00:10:25,135 --> 00:10:27,419
nobody knows what happens at the gate.

314
00:10:27,419 --> 00:10:29,555
The woman told him to keep quiet until it

315
00:10:29,555 --> 00:10:32,193
was too late for you to change your minds.

316
00:10:32,193 --> 00:10:35,031
"I told her you wouldn't," Wes says.

317
00:10:35,031 --> 00:10:36,646
"She said everybody does."

318
00:10:36,646 --> 00:10:38,108
Nora studies him.

319
00:10:38,658 --> 00:10:39,948
"So why tell us?"

320
00:10:39,948 --> 00:10:43,094
"Two months alone on a roof," says Wes.

321
00:10:43,094 --> 00:10:45,923
"I'm out of practice at keeping quiet."

322
00:10:46,506 --> 00:10:48,370
Nobody changes their mind.

323
00:10:48,370 --> 00:10:50,020
But that night,

324
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:52,759
Nora sleeps with the flashlight in her hand.

325
00:10:53,406 --> 00:10:56,184
Day 78 brings more of them to the gate.

326
00:10:56,184 --> 00:10:58,579
The river's early strays are arriving,

327
00:10:58,579 --> 00:10:59,609
and the Doorman's

328
00:10:59,609 --> 00:11:02,096
crowd is twice the size it was.

329
00:11:02,096 --> 00:11:04,351
That night, the pounding changes.

330
00:11:04,351 --> 00:11:06,268
It falls into a rhythm,

331
00:11:06,268 --> 00:11:08,180
as if they are all leaning at once.

332
00:11:08,180 --> 00:11:10,809
Then a dull clank, and the gate moves.

333
00:11:10,809 --> 00:11:12,901
You learn something about oil.

334
00:11:12,901 --> 00:11:14,692
It works for everyone.

335
00:11:14,692 --> 00:11:17,704
The bolt has jumped out of its hole,

336
00:11:17,704 --> 00:11:19,629
and the gate that used to scream now

337
00:11:19,629 --> 00:11:22,751
slides open with a polite little whisper.

338
00:11:22,751 --> 00:11:23,887
A hand's width.

339
00:11:23,887 --> 00:11:24,738
Then two.

340
00:11:25,274 --> 00:11:26,840
You and Wes go down the ladder

341
00:11:26,840 --> 00:11:27,497
in the dark,

342
00:11:27,497 --> 00:11:30,489
while Nora holds the flashlight from the gantry.

343
00:11:30,489 --> 00:11:31,828
Green fingers are already

344
00:11:31,828 --> 00:11:34,128
curling around the edge of the gate.

345
00:11:34,128 --> 00:11:36,128
Among them, an olive sleeve.

346
00:11:36,128 --> 00:11:37,465
Of course.

347
00:11:37,465 --> 00:11:39,440
You throw your weight against the panel.

348
00:11:39,440 --> 00:11:40,934
It slides back slowly,

349
00:11:40,934 --> 00:11:44,628
then slower, and the fingers slip out at the last moment.

350
00:11:44,628 --> 00:11:47,752
Wes stamps the bolt down into its hole.

351
00:11:47,752 --> 00:11:49,875
On day 79, you find a steel bar in

352
00:11:49,875 --> 00:11:51,554
the maintenance cupboard and wedge

353
00:11:51,554 --> 00:11:53,623
it into the rail beside the wheel.

354
00:11:53,623 --> 00:11:55,122
Now the gate can't move

355
00:11:55,122 --> 00:11:57,439
until somebody lifts it out.

356
00:11:57,439 --> 00:11:59,586
The Doorman pounds on it all day,

357
00:11:59,586 --> 00:12:00,959
deeply offended.

358
00:12:01,896 --> 00:12:04,243
On day 80, Nora opens the control box.

359
00:12:04,243 --> 00:12:05,849
Next to the switch,

360
00:12:05,849 --> 00:12:07,514
a small battery light glows.

361
00:12:07,514 --> 00:12:08,848
It is half green.

362
00:12:09,592 --> 00:12:11,341
"Half isn't enough," she says.

363
00:12:11,341 --> 00:12:13,330
"It has to call all morning."

364
00:12:14,091 --> 00:12:16,203
The solar panel on the canopy roof has

365
00:12:16,203 --> 00:12:18,971
spent two months collecting dust and birds.

366
00:12:18,971 --> 00:12:20,364
Somebody has to clean it.

367
00:12:20,364 --> 00:12:22,457
The canopy roof is thin metal,

368
00:12:22,457 --> 00:12:24,371
and right under it sits the coach.

369
00:12:24,371 --> 00:12:26,067
You climb over the gantry rail

370
00:12:26,067 --> 00:12:28,387
with a cloth and a bottle of water.

371
00:12:28,387 --> 00:12:30,491
You crawl on your hands and knees,

372
00:12:30,491 --> 00:12:31,704
spreading your weight,

373
00:12:31,704 --> 00:12:34,431
the way you would cross ice you didn't trust.

374
00:12:34,804 --> 00:12:37,558
Every time the roof flexes, it booms.

375
00:12:37,931 --> 00:12:40,441
Below you, the passengers notice.

376
00:12:40,441 --> 00:12:42,077
Eleven green faces tilt

377
00:12:42,077 --> 00:12:43,881
up at the coach windows.

378
00:12:43,881 --> 00:12:45,577
The man in the blue vest

379
00:12:45,577 --> 00:12:47,150
presses against the glass.

380
00:12:47,150 --> 00:12:48,826
The whole coach rocks once

381
00:12:48,826 --> 00:12:50,836
on its springs, like a sigh.

382
00:12:51,338 --> 00:12:53,174
So you clean slowly.

383
00:12:53,174 --> 00:12:54,893
One strip a day.

384
00:12:55,315 --> 00:12:57,020
On day 83, the light in the

385
00:12:57,020 --> 00:12:59,207
control box turns fully green.

386
00:12:59,901 --> 00:13:03,255
"Now it can shout all day," says Nora.

387
00:13:03,255 --> 00:13:04,884
"Good," says Wes.

388
00:13:04,884 --> 00:13:06,266
"Somebody should."

389
00:13:06,266 --> 00:13:08,140
On the night of day 84,

390
00:13:08,140 --> 00:13:11,684
you hear something you have never heard before.

391
00:13:12,026 --> 00:13:14,925
It comes from far away, beyond the hill.

392
00:13:15,299 --> 00:13:15,884
A low,

393
00:13:15,884 --> 00:13:20,200
endless rustle, like the sea, or like traffic on a

394
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:22,100
road that has no traffic anymore.

395
00:13:22,100 --> 00:13:23,563
It doesn't rise.

396
00:13:23,563 --> 00:13:24,798
It doesn't fall.

397
00:13:24,798 --> 00:13:26,479
It just continues.

398
00:13:27,158 --> 00:13:29,270
"That's them," Wes whispers.

399
00:13:29,270 --> 00:13:30,587
"That's feet."

400
00:13:31,410 --> 00:13:32,571
At dawn on day 85,

401
00:13:32,571 --> 00:13:34,583
you see it from the gantry.

402
00:13:35,021 --> 00:13:36,818
Far off, past the rooftops,

403
00:13:36,818 --> 00:13:40,043
a dark line that moves when nothing else does.

404
00:13:40,043 --> 00:13:41,643
The radio crackles.

405
00:13:42,001 --> 00:13:44,416
"Five days," says the woman.

406
00:13:44,416 --> 00:13:45,867
"Maybe four.

407
00:13:45,867 --> 00:13:48,268
The river doesn't keep a schedule."

408
00:13:48,268 --> 00:13:50,680
Then something happens at your own

409
00:13:50,680 --> 00:13:52,953
gate that hasn't happened in weeks.

410
00:13:53,391 --> 00:13:54,685
The pounding stops.

411
00:13:54,685 --> 00:13:55,462
One by one,

412
00:13:55,462 --> 00:13:58,502
the Doorman's crowd turns away and starts walking

413
00:13:58,502 --> 00:14:00,589
up the street, toward the sound.

414
00:14:01,011 --> 00:14:03,138
Even the Doorman goes.

415
00:14:03,138 --> 00:14:06,417
On day 86, your gate has nobody behind it.

416
00:14:06,417 --> 00:14:07,373
It is the most

417
00:14:07,373 --> 00:14:10,901
frightening silence of the month.

418
00:14:10,901 --> 00:14:14,003
Days 87, 88 and 89 are for

419
00:14:14,003 --> 00:14:16,323
checking things that are already checked.

420
00:14:16,825 --> 00:14:19,295
The steel bar comes out of the gate rail.

421
00:14:19,295 --> 00:14:21,143
The wheel gets more oil,

422
00:14:21,143 --> 00:14:23,540
though the gate already slides like a rumor.

423
00:14:23,945 --> 00:14:25,489
The orange bag gets water,

424
00:14:25,489 --> 00:14:27,890
crackers and the blue bandages,

425
00:14:27,890 --> 00:14:30,176
because it goes wherever you go.

426
00:14:30,176 --> 00:14:31,447
Wes rehearses the

427
00:14:31,447 --> 00:14:33,260
switch with his eyes shut.

428
00:14:33,260 --> 00:14:35,697
On the last night, you and Nora sit on

429
00:14:35,697 --> 00:14:37,920
the bench with the lantern turned low.

430
00:14:38,470 --> 00:14:39,848
"The rule," she says.

431
00:14:39,848 --> 00:14:42,299
"If they come toward the gate,

432
00:14:42,299 --> 00:14:43,356
you close it."

433
00:14:43,356 --> 00:14:45,467
"Even if you're outside."

434
00:14:45,824 --> 00:14:47,365
"Even if I'm outside."

435
00:14:47,365 --> 00:14:50,290
She doesn't say another word all evening.

436
00:14:50,290 --> 00:14:52,421
You have learned what that means.

437
00:14:52,730 --> 00:14:53,601
On the stairs,

438
00:14:53,601 --> 00:14:56,736
Wes pretends to be asleep, and does it badly.

439
00:14:56,736 --> 00:14:57,987
He forgets to snore.

440
00:14:57,987 --> 00:15:01,143
Day 90 starts with the sea in the streets.

441
00:15:01,143 --> 00:15:04,169
The rustle is under every other sound now.

442
00:15:04,169 --> 00:15:06,142
Nora climbs to the far end of

443
00:15:06,142 --> 00:15:08,034
the gantry with the flashlight.

444
00:15:08,034 --> 00:15:10,015
Wes opens the control box.

445
00:15:10,015 --> 00:15:11,428
You climb down the ladder,

446
00:15:11,428 --> 00:15:13,857
lift the bolt and slide the gate open.

447
00:15:13,857 --> 00:15:15,398
It barely whispers.

448
00:15:15,398 --> 00:15:18,000
Then you step into the slot between

449
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:19,117
the panel and the wall,

450
00:15:19,117 --> 00:15:22,628
with the orange bag held against your chest, and you wait.

451
00:15:22,628 --> 00:15:23,422
From the slot,

452
00:15:23,422 --> 00:15:26,273
you can see one narrow strip of street.

453
00:15:26,273 --> 00:15:27,982
For an hour, nothing.

454
00:15:28,660 --> 00:15:29,019
Then,

455
00:15:29,019 --> 00:15:32,070
around the corner, one short blast of a horn.

456
00:15:32,524 --> 00:15:34,029
Above you, a click.

457
00:15:34,029 --> 00:15:37,102
And for the first time in sixty-two days,

458
00:15:37,102 --> 00:15:39,710
a voice you know rolls across the yard.

459
00:15:39,710 --> 00:15:41,550
"Passengers for Platform 1."

460
00:15:41,550 --> 00:15:42,854
The first ones come

461
00:15:42,854 --> 00:15:45,166
through the gate almost at once.

462
00:15:45,166 --> 00:15:46,001
Then more.

463
00:15:46,001 --> 00:15:47,443
You stop counting.

464
00:15:47,443 --> 00:15:48,903
No number would help.

465
00:15:48,903 --> 00:15:50,687
Legs pass the edge of the slot,

466
00:15:50,687 --> 00:15:52,547
close enough to touch.

467
00:15:52,547 --> 00:15:53,969
Shoulders brush the panel.

468
00:15:53,969 --> 00:15:56,339
The metal hums against your back.

469
00:15:56,339 --> 00:15:57,410
Nobody looks in.

470
00:15:57,410 --> 00:16:00,464
The river pours into the yard all morning.

471
00:16:00,464 --> 00:16:01,700
It fills the concrete from

472
00:16:01,700 --> 00:16:03,419
the canopy almost to the gate.

473
00:16:03,826 --> 00:16:05,284
It packs around the coach,

474
00:16:05,284 --> 00:16:08,108
where eleven passengers watch through the glass

475
00:16:08,108 --> 00:16:10,011
like the only ones who bought tickets.

476
00:16:10,011 --> 00:16:11,820
It stands under the canopy with its

477
00:16:11,820 --> 00:16:12,715
faces turned up,

478
00:16:12,715 --> 00:16:16,419
listening to an announcement for a bus that is never coming.

479
00:16:16,419 --> 00:16:18,736
At midday, the voice stops.

480
00:16:18,736 --> 00:16:20,288
Wes has switched it off.

481
00:16:20,288 --> 00:16:21,406
The yard is full.

482
00:16:21,406 --> 00:16:22,586
The river is not.

483
00:16:22,586 --> 00:16:25,666
Late on day 90, the tail is still coming.

484
00:16:25,992 --> 00:16:27,152
Slower now.

485
00:16:27,152 --> 00:16:28,313
Stragglers.

486
00:16:28,313 --> 00:16:30,257
Twos and threes.

487
00:16:30,792 --> 00:16:31,910
Up on the gantry,

488
00:16:31,910 --> 00:16:33,950
a small light flashes once.

489
00:16:34,324 --> 00:16:34,904
Close it.

490
00:16:34,904 --> 00:16:37,049
You reach for the edge of the panel.

491
00:16:37,049 --> 00:16:39,208
And there is always one more,

492
00:16:39,208 --> 00:16:41,027
stepping into the gap.

493
00:16:41,027 --> 00:16:43,172
And one more behind that one.

494
00:16:43,172 --> 00:16:45,623
The tail is longer than anyone said.

495
00:16:45,623 --> 00:16:48,177
It will not end while the gate is open.

496
00:16:48,177 --> 00:16:49,878
And the gate will not

497
00:16:49,878 --> 00:16:51,307
close while it keeps coming.

498
00:16:51,307 --> 00:16:52,918
So you do the one thing Nora

499
00:16:52,918 --> 00:16:54,842
asked you never to do, on day 4,

500
00:16:54,842 --> 00:16:57,402
the first time she ever spoke to you.

501
00:16:57,402 --> 00:16:58,610
You slip out of the slot,

502
00:16:58,610 --> 00:17:01,299
through the gateway and into the street.

503
00:17:01,299 --> 00:17:02,219
Between them.

504
00:17:02,219 --> 00:17:03,073
Around them.

505
00:17:03,073 --> 00:17:04,260
Ten steps.

506
00:17:04,260 --> 00:17:04,593
Twenty.

507
00:17:04,593 --> 00:17:05,842
Then you turn,

508
00:17:05,842 --> 00:17:08,493
fill your lungs, and shout.

509
00:17:08,493 --> 00:17:09,616
"Over here!"

510
00:17:09,616 --> 00:17:12,610
Every head in the gateway turns.

511
00:17:12,610 --> 00:17:14,605
Every head in the street turns.

512
00:17:14,605 --> 00:17:15,492
For ninety days,

513
00:17:15,492 --> 00:17:18,633
you have tried very hard not to be interesting.

514
00:17:19,024 --> 00:17:20,140
Now you are the most

515
00:17:20,140 --> 00:17:22,087
interesting thing in the city.

516
00:17:22,461 --> 00:17:24,983
They come out of the gateway, toward you.

517
00:17:24,983 --> 00:17:26,004
The last one out

518
00:17:26,004 --> 00:17:27,875
wears a ragged olive vest.

519
00:17:27,875 --> 00:17:30,599
Behind him, through the narrowing gap,

520
00:17:30,599 --> 00:17:33,957
you see an arm with a purple band, pushing.

521
00:17:34,412 --> 00:17:36,867
The gate slides shut with a whisper.

522
00:17:36,867 --> 00:17:38,229
The bolt drops.

523
00:17:38,684 --> 00:17:40,264
Nora kept the rule.

524
00:17:40,264 --> 00:17:42,003
You made her promise.

525
00:17:42,003 --> 00:17:43,746
You back up the street,

526
00:17:43,746 --> 00:17:46,133
and the tail follows, and the top of

527
00:17:46,133 --> 00:17:48,891
the street is only more street.

528
00:17:48,891 --> 00:17:50,834
Then an engine, close.

529
00:17:51,449 --> 00:17:53,350
The white truck swings out of a side

530
00:17:53,350 --> 00:17:55,897
road with its cab door already open.

531
00:17:55,897 --> 00:17:57,456
A hand reaches out.

532
00:17:57,456 --> 00:17:58,380
"Get in."

533
00:17:59,397 --> 00:18:00,416
You get in.

534
00:18:00,416 --> 00:18:01,775
The door slams.

535
00:18:01,775 --> 00:18:04,427
The truck rolls, and the tail of

536
00:18:04,427 --> 00:18:06,567
the river turns to follow the engine.

537
00:18:07,101 --> 00:18:08,733
In the gloom of the cab,

538
00:18:08,733 --> 00:18:12,325
the woman is only a shape with two hands on the wheel.

539
00:18:12,325 --> 00:18:13,764
"Wes said you were the

540
00:18:13,764 --> 00:18:16,238
careful one," she says.

541
00:18:16,238 --> 00:18:16,973
"I am."

542
00:18:16,973 --> 00:18:20,040
"Then I'd hate to meet the other one."

543
00:18:20,040 --> 00:18:21,871
She doesn't drive toward the hill.

544
00:18:21,871 --> 00:18:23,779
Not with that behind you.

545
00:18:23,779 --> 00:18:25,211
She drives the other way,

546
00:18:25,211 --> 00:18:27,609
into streets you have never seen.

547
00:18:27,609 --> 00:18:29,685
At the corner, you look back.

548
00:18:30,123 --> 00:18:31,882
Above the depot wall hangs

549
00:18:31,882 --> 00:18:33,875
a sign that says THREE ALIVE.

550
00:18:34,362 --> 00:18:36,759
Tonight, only two of them are behind it.

551
00:18:36,759 --> 00:18:39,296
And at the far end of the gantry,

552
00:18:39,296 --> 00:18:40,861
a small light blinks once.

553
00:18:40,861 --> 00:18:41,856
Still here.

554
00:18:42,246 --> 00:18:42,964
Ninety days.

555
00:18:42,964 --> 00:18:45,260
The same orange bandage.

556
00:18:45,618 --> 00:18:47,425
Nora is holding the flashlight.

557
00:18:47,425 --> 00:18:49,616
You are holding the orange bag.

558
